WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.630
We introduce apparent ridges
for non-photo realistic line

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:08.470
drawings.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.200
They are view dependent
extensions of ridge and valley

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.580
lines.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.710
Given an object,
a viewing screen,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.330
and a projection from
the object to the screen,

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.180
we define view
dependent curvature

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.070
as the change in
normal of the object

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.830
as one moves a small
distance in the screen.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.590
Note that this value
increases towards the contours

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.780
because it takes
foreshortening of the object

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.400
into consideration.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.760
Here we see curvature and
view dependent curvature color

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.650
coded onto the object.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.350
The warmer the color,
the higher the value.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.180
On front facing
parts of the image,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.070
the two values are similar.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.990
When the object turns
away from the viewer,

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.810
the view dependent
curvature gets much larger.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.910
Apparent ridges are the
extrema of this view

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:49.960
dependent curvature.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.670
We compare apparent
ridges to other lines.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.550
Apparent ridges are related
to ridges and valleys.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.380
Where ridges and
valleys do well,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.140
apparent ridges are
in similar locations.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.080
Where ridge and valley lines
are too rigid and boxy,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.020
apparent ridges modify them
to be closer to the contours.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.320
While ridge and valley lines
are fixed to the surface,

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.560
apparent ridges slide
smoothly along the surface.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.180
Apparent ridges are red.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.310
Ridges and valleys
are purple and brown.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.250
Apparent ridges are different
from suggestive contours.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.410
Both produce pleasing
images, but we

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:46.040
find that apparent ridges are
more appealing in many cases.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.900
They produce more natural
images for facial features,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.720
and include lines in convex
regions where suggestive

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.150
contours cannot appear.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.770
Line drawings look
better when they

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.810
are thresholded appropriately.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.330
Here we compare successive
thresholding levels

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.200
for the three different
feature lines.

